WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.888 --> 00:00:05.400
This is Greenroom: open-source speaker
and session management for conferences.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.300
Call for papers, reviews, speaker
onboarding, agenda, emails,

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.300
and the public program. One app,
open source, yours to deploy.

4
00:00:15.613 --> 00:00:17.600
Here's a demo.

5
00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:22.600
We're running AI Engineer Summit 2026, and
its call for speakers is open right now.

6
00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.600
In the next ten minutes,
one talk goes from unknown

7
00:00:25.794 --> 00:00:28.500
to published, with a calendar
invite in the speaker's inbox.

8
00:00:28.696 --> 00:00:34.900
Organizers tweak their CFP forms constantly.
In most tools, every tweak is a support ticket.

9
00:00:35.211 --> 00:00:38.700
Here, the form is data:
questions, conditions, welcome copy,

10
00:00:38.868 --> 00:00:41.500
the confirmation email, and the
public link, all on one screen.

11
00:00:41.796 --> 00:00:45.900
Every call for speakers this event runs.
Open the main one.

12
00:00:46.303 --> 00:00:49.800
Here's a question called Workshop
requirements, with a Conditional badge.

13
00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:52.800
The rule: when Session format
is a 90-minute workshop.

14
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:58.100
It only appears when a speaker picks
workshop. Built in the UI, nothing deployed.

15
00:00:58.384 --> 00:01:02.700
Most talks have two people on stage, and
most tools treat the second as an afterthought.

16
00:01:02.958 --> 00:01:06.900
Here, co-speakers are part of the
proposal, and every email goes to both.

17
00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:10.700
The confirmation email is the organizer's
own words: subject and body.

18
00:01:10.975 --> 00:01:14.300
Merge fields fill in with
real values when it sends.

19
00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:17.300
And a live preview of what
the speaker will read.

20
00:01:17.563 --> 00:01:22.400
The public link, when it opens, and
when it closes. Same screen.

21
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.500
Save. Nothing deployed.
The form is live for speakers.

22
00:01:25.761 --> 00:01:29.000
Now the speaker's side: no login
wall, no account to create.

23
00:01:29.208 --> 00:01:31.600
That wall is where CFP conversion dies.

24
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.700
The intro sets the bar:
practitioner talks,

25
00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:38.600
things you built, shipped, measured,
and would do differently.

26
00:01:38.864 --> 00:01:42.400
A title, an abstract, then the
organizer's questions, in her order.

27
00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:46.600
Tracks are a real question:
the track decides who reviews it.

28
00:01:46.912 --> 00:01:49.700
Pick 90-minute workshop, and
Workshop requirements appears.

29
00:01:49.897 --> 00:01:53.900
That's the conditional rule from a
minute ago, working on the public form.

30
00:01:54.224 --> 00:01:58.500
Switch to a 45-minute talk,
and it disappears again.

31
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.400
Co-speakers are on,
so Nadia adds Owen.

32
00:02:04.706 --> 00:02:08.100
Proposal received, with the
organizer's own confirmation copy.

33
00:02:08.306 --> 00:02:10.800
A confirmation email is
on its way to Nadia.

34
00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.100
There it is: two emails,
one per speaker.

35
00:02:13.282 --> 00:02:17.900
The subject the organizer wrote, merge
fields filled in with the real values.

36
00:02:18.241 --> 00:02:21.200
Not a queue to drain later.
Already sent.

37
00:02:21.481 --> 00:02:24.800
Nadia can edit her proposal
from that email. No password.

38
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:29.200
Back in the organizer's seat: every proposal,
one queue, statuses in plain language.

39
00:02:29.392 --> 00:02:32.700
Filter to Unreviewed:
the new talk is at the top,

40
00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:37.500
Nadia and Owen both on it,
routed to Evals & Reliability.

41
00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:46.800
Every answer, exactly as submitted,

42
00:02:46.947 --> 00:02:49.700
including the co-speaker: Owen Diallo,
Staff Engineer at Waypoint.

43
00:02:49.892 --> 00:02:53.000
Now, something every program chair
needs forty times per CFP.

44
00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.500
This abstract is nearly right. I don't
want to accept it or reject it.

45
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.800
I want to ask for one change, and
I want that ask to be an email.

46
00:03:01.016 --> 00:03:03.700
Request changes emails the speaker
what to fix, with an edit link.

47
00:03:03.832 --> 00:03:05.900
The submission keeps its current status.

48
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:08.400
A note on what to change,
and a deadline.

49
00:03:08.603 --> 00:03:10.100
Change request sent.

50
00:03:10.216 --> 00:03:13.500
A real email: "A quick change
needed" on her talk,

51
00:03:13.661 --> 00:03:17.600
with the deadline, and a link that
opens her proposal ready to edit.

52
00:03:17.809 --> 00:03:19.400
And the status: still Unreviewed.

53
00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:23.500
Asking for a fix isn't a decision,
so it doesn't pretend to be one.

54
00:03:33.022 --> 00:03:35.100
Review rounds: assign
the talk to Dana.

55
00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:39.800
That named assignment, not broad track
access, is what lets her score it.

56
00:03:40.009 --> 00:03:44.400
It's now in Dana's
First-pass review queue.

57
00:03:44.581 --> 00:03:48.400
Dana reviews Evals & Reliability.
Marco reviews Agents & Tool Use.

58
00:03:48.619 --> 00:03:51.800
Routing by track has to
work in both directions.

59
00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:57.100
Her track list is readable context, identities
hidden, but only assigned talks can be scored.

60
00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.100
One Agents & Tool Use talk
isn't in her list at all.

61
00:04:00.253 --> 00:04:03.800
The hard version: paste that
talk's URL into Dana's browser.

62
00:04:04.015 --> 00:04:08.100
Not "access denied", not greyed out.
For Dana, the talk does not exist.

63
00:04:08.298 --> 00:04:10.500
Her assigned talk:
Score this submission.

64
00:04:10.694 --> 00:04:14.700
The round is blind, so the scorecard
opens without the speaker's identity.

65
00:04:14.948 --> 00:04:18.300
Dana judges the proposal,
not the name behind it.

66
00:04:18.631 --> 00:04:21.700
Two ratings, the round's own
vocabulary, and her reasoning.

67
00:04:21.867 --> 00:04:25.500
Every answer belongs to this
assignment and this scorecard.

68
00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:29.000
Submitted. Her queue records
the work as complete.

69
00:04:29.219 --> 00:04:31.800
The proposal now carries
her filed scorecard.

70
00:04:32.029 --> 00:04:35.100
The final decision belongs to an
event admin. Scorecards feed it.

71
00:04:35.306 --> 00:04:38.300
Dana can't accept it herself.

72
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:43.200
Accepting creates a session and emails a
promise to a human. That's the organizer's call.

73
00:04:44.852 --> 00:04:47.700
Back as the organizer: Dana's
scorecard is on the record,

74
00:04:47.836 --> 00:04:50.400
her ratings, the round's Accept,
and her reasoning.

75
00:04:50.588 --> 00:04:53.000
A note to the speakers,
which goes into the email.

76
00:04:53.178 --> 00:04:55.300
Email the speakers stays ticked.

77
00:04:55.469 --> 00:05:00.500
Accept creates the session, assigns the
checklist, and emails everyone on the talk.

78
00:05:00.722 --> 00:05:04.000
Session created: twelve tasks across
two speakers, automatically.

79
00:05:04.126 --> 00:05:07.400
Six each, because the talk has
a co-speaker. Two emails out.

80
00:05:07.571 --> 00:05:10.500
That's the handoff from program
committee to speaker ops,

81
00:05:10.723 --> 00:05:15.200
the part conferences run on a spreadsheet
and a prayer, collapsed into one click.

82
00:05:15.422 --> 00:05:18.700
On the record: accepted by
Avery Chen, note echoed below.

83
00:05:18.898 --> 00:05:21.000
Session created,
not yet on the agenda.

84
00:05:21.197 --> 00:05:23.200
Twelve tasks across two speakers.

85
00:05:23.426 --> 00:05:25.800
What it doesn't do:
guess a room and a time.

86
00:05:25.989 --> 00:05:28.500
An accepted talk stays unscheduled
until a human places it.

87
00:05:28.693 --> 00:05:31.400
The email carries the committee's
note, word for word.

88
00:05:31.635 --> 00:05:35.100
And a "Still outstanding" list:
all six tasks, with due dates.

89
00:05:35.291 --> 00:05:41.500
The checklist is in the email itself, so it
works even for speakers who never open the portal.

90
00:05:48.113 --> 00:05:52.900
Everyone signs in the same way:
a magic link. No passwords anywhere.

91
00:05:53.073 --> 00:05:54.600
In production that's an email.

92
00:05:54.674 --> 00:05:58.500
In this demo, the dev transport writes
it to a file. Watch the terminal.

93
00:05:58.749 --> 00:06:00.500
And she's in: her speaker home.

94
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.700
Her submission, badged Approved.

95
00:06:02.896 --> 00:06:06.500
Her session, not yet scheduled.
Honest about the state of the world.

96
00:06:06.754 --> 00:06:08.900
And six tasks from that
one Accept click.

97
00:06:09.066 --> 00:06:12.300
Talk description (already overdue),
bio and photos, flights,

98
00:06:12.428 --> 00:06:15.100
announcements, the speaker
discount, and the hotel form.

99
00:06:15.285 --> 00:06:17.900
The six things every conference
chases every speaker for.

100
00:06:18.101 --> 00:06:21.900
They exist because the talk was
accepted. Nobody typed them in.

101
00:06:22.354 --> 00:06:24.600
The hotel form: say yes,
and date fields appear.

102
00:06:24.791 --> 00:06:29.400
Same conditional-form engine as the
CFP. One form system, everywhere.

103
00:06:30.146 --> 00:06:33.700
Submitted inline. The
task flips to Complete.

104
00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:39.700
The other types are just as blunt: one
is "Mark as done", one wants a file.

105
00:06:39.957 --> 00:06:42.500
The organizer sees that
submission immediately:

106
00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:46.100
Nadia's completion meter, her
overdue count, a square per task.

107
00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:49.100
Other speakers sit anywhere from
nothing done to all done.

108
00:06:49.274 --> 00:06:53.700
This screen replaces the "who still
hasn't sent a headshot" spreadsheet.

109
00:06:54.286 --> 00:06:58.100
The agenda: drag sessions onto a room and
time. Conflicts are flagged, never blocked.

110
00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.600
No conflicts so far.
Let's cause one.

111
00:07:00.736 --> 00:07:05.100
The tray holds two unscheduled cards, including
the new talk with Nadia and Owen on it.

112
00:07:05.302 --> 00:07:09.700
Day one already has a talk
on Main Stage at ten.

113
00:07:11.554 --> 00:07:13.900
Drop the new talk
straight on top of it.

114
00:07:14.034 --> 00:07:17.000
Watch what it doesn't do:
it doesn't refuse the drop.

115
00:07:17.144 --> 00:07:20.800
Organizers double-book on purpose. It takes
the drop, then tells the truth about it.

116
00:07:20.994 --> 00:07:24.100
Both cards outline in red:
room double-booked.

117
00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.900
And the summary now
reads "1 conflict".

118
00:07:27.149 --> 00:07:34.500
It names both talks, same room, same
time, with a link straight to the day.

119
00:07:34.836 --> 00:07:36.300
Reload: still there.

120
00:07:36.438 --> 00:07:40.700
A conflict is never a reason
to drop your change.

121
00:07:41.321 --> 00:07:46.200
Move it to Workshop B at one o'clock:
the red clears, and the badge goes quiet.

122
00:07:46.456 --> 00:07:49.100
Double-booked rooms and speakers
are hard conflicts.

123
00:07:49.314 --> 00:07:52.100
Two same-track talks head-to-head
is an amber advisory,

124
00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.900
flagged because attendees hate it,
but never treated as an error.

125
00:07:57.673 --> 00:07:59.800
The Log: every message
this event has sent.

126
00:07:59.943 --> 00:08:03.100
The acceptance and the change
request are already in it.

127
00:08:03.264 --> 00:08:04.900
Filter by speaker or message type.

128
00:08:05.032 --> 00:08:07.100
Three things conferences
actually pay for.

129
00:08:07.218 --> 00:08:09.400
First: task reminders
that don't spam.

130
00:08:09.547 --> 00:08:15.100
One digest per speaker per week, listing
everything still open. Not one email per task.

131
00:08:15.263 --> 00:08:20.500
The toast reports what it did and what it
deliberately skipped, a reason for every skip.

132
00:08:20.686 --> 00:08:22.800
That's a cron job you can trust.

133
00:08:22.966 --> 00:08:25.600
Reload: the button is disabled.

134
00:08:25.823 --> 00:08:28.600
Everyone was just emailed, and a 24-hour
guard blocks the second nag.

135
00:08:28.807 --> 00:08:30.900
Second: the wording is yours.

136
00:08:31.091 --> 00:08:34.900
Twelve built-in messages, from Submission
received to Calendar invitation.

137
00:08:35.161 --> 00:08:39.400
Type "See you in session room" and it's refused.
The field is real, but a reminder can't fill it.

138
00:08:39.565 --> 00:08:41.000
Save is disabled.

139
00:08:41.166 --> 00:08:43.400
It would arrive as a blank
space in an inbox.

140
00:08:43.604 --> 00:08:45.900
Nobody re-reads sent mail,
so it's caught here.

141
00:08:46.207 --> 00:08:48.900
Replace it with something
fillable, and it saves.

142
00:08:49.106 --> 00:08:50.500
An "Edited" badge appears.

143
00:08:50.708 --> 00:08:54.800
That's this event's wording: an override,
not a fork. Other events keep the default.

144
00:08:55.071 --> 00:08:57.300
One click back.
No painted corners.

145
00:08:57.549 --> 00:09:00.500
Third: calendar invites that
update instead of duplicating.

146
00:09:00.768 --> 00:09:05.300
The new talk's row shows its slot,
Workshop B, and both speakers.

147
00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.000
Invitation sent to both speakers.

148
00:09:07.219 --> 00:09:12.800
A real invite: method REQUEST, sequence zero,
a stable UID, and the room as the location.

149
00:09:13.017 --> 00:09:17.100
It lands in Google Calendar and Outlook
as an event, not an attachment.

150
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.300
Reload: invites sent, with
a last-sent timestamp.

151
00:09:20.551 --> 00:09:23.400
Re-send, and the existing calendar
entry updates in place.

152
00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:25.000
Same UID, now sequence one.

153
00:09:25.181 --> 00:09:32.800
Rooms change. When they do, the speaker's
calendar entry moves. No second invite to delete.

154
00:09:33.382 --> 00:09:37.500
The public program: the event,
the dates, the venue,

155
00:09:37.723 --> 00:09:40.900
and every open call for
speakers, linked right there.

156
00:09:41.099 --> 00:09:43.400
Day one: there's the new talk.

157
00:09:43.536 --> 00:09:46.500
One to one forty-five, Workshop B,
Nadia and Owen.

158
00:09:46.645 --> 00:09:48.300
Minutes ago, that was a card I dragged.

159
00:09:48.495 --> 00:09:51.100
No publish step, no export,
no second CMS.

160
00:09:51.311 --> 00:09:53.700
And she's on the speaker wall.

161
00:09:53.891 --> 00:09:56.900
Embed this page: script, iframe,
JSON, XML, and iCal feeds.

162
00:09:57.128 --> 00:09:59.000
Five ways to put this
schedule anywhere.

163
00:09:59.176 --> 00:10:01.500
Copy the iframe. That's
the whole integration.

164
00:10:01.774 --> 00:10:07.400
Paste it into the conference's marketing
site, and the schedule there stays live.

165
00:10:07.614 --> 00:10:10.500
So: one talk, ten minutes.

166
00:10:10.682 --> 00:10:13.000
Proposed by someone with no account.

167
00:10:13.162 --> 00:10:16.300
Routed to the right reviewer,
invisible to the wrong one.

168
00:10:16.523 --> 00:10:18.700
Sent back for a fix, then
accepted with feedback.

169
00:10:18.877 --> 00:10:21.300
Turned into a session and
twelve tasks nobody typed.

170
00:10:21.524 --> 00:10:24.500
Scheduled through a conflict and
invited to the calendar.

171
00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.900
Published to a page you
can embed anywhere.

172
00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.400
Greenroom is open source,
on Cloudflare Workers.

173
00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.600
The data layer is storage-agnostic:

174
00:10:32.788 --> 00:10:35.300
support a new datastore by
writing one adapter.

175
00:10:35.561 --> 00:10:37.233
Thanks for watching.
